{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh7DC7GPtd98CZ6xpZrn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The feature condition is still **not met**. This round closed several smaller gaps, and the four remaining items are larger builds I haven't started.\n\n**Done this round**\n- **Truck unloading:** a truck that stops at the rail unloads four soldiers, once.\n- **Cargo-plane drops:** operation 6 drops groups of paratroopers from the plane into the fight.\n- **Cutscenes:** a briefing before operation 1 and a takeoff cut before operation 6, on top of the interrogation and Kennel cuts.\n- **Awards:** the host's report now names a sharpshooter, a guardian (most projectiles shot down), and a lifesaver (most guards taken out). Each is included only when someone earned it.\n- **Voice:** each letter of a radio line now plays its own pitch, so the blips follow the text. This is still not gibberish speech.\n- **Checks:** all 85 Node checks still pass. The bundle and `exit-interview.json` are rebuilt and valid.\n\n**Still missing against TASK.md**\n- **Mode 7 and the rig system:** not used. The art is a depth-scaled pixel painter with procedural sprites.\n- **Operation 2 rail:** the river still scrolls sideways, not forward along a river.\n- **Bosses:** each has a volley and one special. The plan's phases, weak points, and per-boss attacks are not built.\n- **Multiplayer:** the host is authoritative, clients apply the latest snapshot without interpolation, and clients send no hit reports.\n- **Voice:** the blips are pitched per letter, not Animalese.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 18:50:13\n- Source: Operation Wolf (Taito, 1987)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: shooter, first-person light-gun rail shooter\n- Perspective: first person on a scripted rail, sideways and in depth\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine 0.85.2 handoff (JavaScript, 480×270 canvas, chip audio); AI Hub client 1.4.0 unmodified; Node vm for tests; Playwright 1.63.0 for screenshots and the harness\n- Art direction: pixel art made in code, depth-scaled, dusk and dawn palettes\n- Animation: procedural (rise and drop from cover, closing rings, recoil); the engine rig system is not used\n- Audio: engine chip synth with SFX and voice blips in code; engine songs\n- Online: AI Hub session, shared world, 1–4 players, host simulates\n- Testing: 85 Node checks, bot playthroughs of all six operations, a two-copy hub session, screenshots, and the AI Hub harness (two players)\n- Research: none of my own; the plan's summary of the source\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- **Issues caught:** about eight in the first build, seven of them in the game. The later expansions added more, and the checks and the harness run caught those too.\n- **Critical:** two would have broken the game in a browser: the canvas drawing at 300×150, and a crash on a deep link into attract. A third was a battle-state name collision, found by the test suite.\n- **Most helpful:** the bot playing each operation to its boss headless, and the AI Hub harness conformance run.\n- **Least helpful:** the stub-buffer draw pass, which only shows drawing doesn't throw.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- **Liked most:** the scene flow, pause, settings, input presets, the arcade table and initials, and the headless loop.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** the canvas CSS, the first frame running before later scripts load, and hash deep links that only apply on a fresh page load.\n- **Built by hand:** the battle rules, the rail and beat generator, telegraph rings, the depth painter, the HUD, the bot, and the session layer.\n- **Hardest:** the API card is long, and the pixel primitives snap to whole pixels.\n- **Changed in the engine:** none.\n- **One change I'd make:** document the canvas-size rule for fixed resolutions, and note that the first frame can run before later scripts load.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- **Liked most:** the plan's exact names, files, tests, and done criteria.\n- **Confusing:** the task and plan together ask for far more than one run can build, and the plan names a different model and effort than the task.\n- **Hardest:** the AI Hub integration and its snapshot design.\n- **Would change:** split the work into milestones with separate done lines, and mark which items are optional.\n\nTo finish, the Mode 7 and rig work is the largest piece, followed by the forward river rail and the boss phases. I can keep going on those in order, or you can `/goal clear` and treat this build as the partial deliverable."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":813,"cache_read_input_tokens":475467,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":813},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"972ca841-0540-4c71-85fe-204eae6bea61","uuid":"efafef9d-2cb0-4c04-b524-1eaea65e346d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:35:13.101Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh7DBpABYvJJzEgt314t"}
